.TH IMG339CTL 1 "October 2026" "img339ctl 0.1.8" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
img339ctl \- configure the Inca IMG\-339 gaming mouse
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B img339ctl
.RI [ options ]
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B img339ctl
configures the Inca IMG\-339 gaming mouse (USB ID 18f8:0fc0 or 18f8:1086)
without the vendor's Windows software: DPI levels, LED colours and effects,
button bindings, the "fire" repeat interval and the wheel function.
.PP
The mouse cannot report its settings back, so
.B img339ctl
keeps them in a profile file and sends the whole profile every time a setting
changes, the same way the vendor software does when you press Apply.
Settings are stored in the mouse and survive unplugging.
.PP
Most functions are carried out by the mouse itself.
Media keys, shortcuts, macros and commands are not: the mouse only reports the
button press, and
.B img339ctl daemon
must be running to perform the action.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-profile " FILE"
Use
.I FILE
instead of the default profile (see
.BR FILES ).
.TP
.BI \-\-device " PATH"
Use the given hidraw node instead of detecting the mouse.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Print the reports that would be sent, one per line in hex, and do not
touch the mouse or save the profile.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help.
Every command also accepts
.BR \-\-help .
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version.
.SH COMMANDS
Commands that change a setting save the profile and send it to the mouse.
.TP
.B status
Show the detected mouse and the current profile and, while the daemon is running,
the current DPI.
.TP
.B apply
Send the profile to the mouse without changing it.
.TP
.B reset
Restore the factory defaults (the same as "Reset" in the vendor software).
.TP
.B actions
List everything a button can be bound to, and the macro syntax.
.TP
.BI "dpi " "LEVEL " \fR[\fIVALUE\fR] " \fR[\fB\-\-on\fR|\fB\-\-off\fR] [\fB\-\-active\fR]"
Set the DPI of
.I LEVEL
(1\-6).
.I VALUE
is one of 200, 400, 600, 800, 1000, 1200, 1600, 2000, 2400, 3200, 4000 or 4800.
.B \-\-off
removes the level from the rotation of the DPI button,
.B \-\-on
puts it back, and
.B \-\-active
makes the mouse start in this level.
At least one level must stay enabled.
.TP
.BI "color " "LEVEL COLOR"
Set the LED colour shown in
.I LEVEL
(1\-6, or
.B all
for every level) when the LED mode is
.B dpi
or
.BR static .
.I COLOR
is
.I RRGGBB
hex (with or without
.BR # ,
3\-digit shorthand allowed) or one of
.BR red ", " green ", " blue ", " yellow ", " cyan ", " magenta ", " pink ,
.BR white ", " orange ", " purple ", " black .
The mouse has 4 bits per channel, so close colours may look the same.
.TP
.BI "button " "N ACTION"
Bind button
.I N
(1\-7, numbered as in the vendor software) to
.IR ACTION .
See
.BR ACTIONS .
.TP
.BI "led " MODE " \fR[\fB\-\-time\fR \fISECONDS\fR] [\fB\-\-colors\fR \fILIST\fR]"
Set the LED mode:
.B dpi
(the colour of the current DPI level),
.B cycle
(cycle through the built\-in colours),
.B static
(one steady colour; set it with
.BR "color all" ),
or
.BR off .
.B \-\-time
sets the cycle period, 1\-6 seconds.
.B \-\-colors
selects which of the 7 built\-in cycle colours are used, for example
.BR \(dq1 3 5\(dq .
.TP
.BI "fire " MS
Set the repeat interval of the
.B fire
function in milliseconds (8\-2040, rounded down to a multiple of 8).
.TP
.BR "wheel scroll" | volume
Make the wheel scroll, or change the volume.
.TP
.BI "macro " NAME " \fR[\fISTEP\fR...]"
Define the macro
.I NAME
from the given steps, or delete it when no steps are given.
See
.BR "MACRO SYNTAX" .
.TP
.B daemon
Apply the profile, then wait for presses of buttons bound to host\-side
actions and carry them out.
Reconnects automatically when the mouse is unplugged and plugged back in.
Run it from your session, for example with the supplied systemd user unit.
.SH ACTIONS
Handled by the mouse itself:
.RS
.B left middle right back forward dpi\-loop show\-desktop double\-click fire off dpi+ dpi\-
.RE
.PP
.B off
disables the button.
.B fire
is the vendor's rapid\-fire function; its repeat interval is set with the
.B fire
command.
.PP
Carried out by
.BR "img339ctl daemon" :
.TP
Media keys
.B browser player mail volume\-up volume\-down previous next mute play\-pause calculator stop
.TP
Shortcuts
.B copy paste cut select\-all undo redo find close\-window
.TP
Commands
.B file\-manager
(runs
.BR "xdg\-open ~" ),
.B lock
(runs
.BR "loginctl lock\-session" )
.TP
.BI keys: CHORD
Press a key combination, e.g.
.BR keys:ctrl+shift+t .
.TP
.BI macro: NAME \fR[\fB:\fIN\fR|\fB:hold\fR]
Play a macro once,
.I N
times, or repeatedly while the button is held.
.TP
.BI run: COMMAND
Run a shell command with
.BR "sh \-c" .
.PP
Keystrokes are sent by the mouse, so they work in every application and
under both X11 and Wayland.
.SH MACRO SYNTAX
A macro is a list of steps separated by spaces:
.TP
.I KEY\fR or \fIKEY\fB+\fIKEY\fR...
Tap a key or a chord (press in order, release in reverse).
.TP
.BI + KEY " \fRand\fB " \- KEY
Hold or release a key.
.TP
.IB N ms "\fR or \fIN"
Wait
.I N
milliseconds.
Steps are otherwise 15 ms apart.
.TP
.BI text: STRING
Type
.I STRING
(letters, digits, space and US\-layout punctuation; no spaces inside one step).
.PP
Key names:
.BR a \- z ,
.BR 0 \- 9 ,
.BR f1 \- f12 ,
.BR enter ", " esc ", " backspace ", " tab ", " space ", " minus ", " equal ,
.BR leftbracket ", " rightbracket ", " backslash ", " semicolon ", " apostrophe ,
.BR grave ", " comma ", " dot ", " slash ", " capslock ", " printscreen ,
.BR scrolllock ", " pause ", " insert ", " home ", " pageup ", " delete ", " end ,
.BR pagedown ", " up ", " down ", " left ", " right ", " menu ,
the modifiers
.BR ctrl ", " shift ", " alt ", " super ", " rctrl ", " rshift ", " altgr ", " rsuper ,
and the mouse buttons
.BR mouse\-left ", " mouse\-right ", " mouse\-middle .
Keys are sent as US\-layout scan codes; the active keyboard layout decides
which character they produce.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/img339ctl/profile.ini
The profile (default
.IR ~/.config/img339ctl/profile.ini ).
It is a plain INI file and can be edited by hand; run
.B img339ctl apply
afterwards.
.TP
.I /etc/udev/rules.d/70\-img339\-mouse.rules
udev rule giving the logged\-in user access to the mouse's hidraw node
(shipped as
.IR contrib/70\-img339\-mouse.rules ).
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/user/img339ctl.service
systemd user unit that runs the daemon, on by default with the Debian package:
it writes the saved profile at login and when the mouse is plugged in, if there is
one. Turn it off with
.BR "systemctl \-\-user mask \-\-now img339ctl.service" .
Other installs can copy
.I contrib/img339ctl.service
to
.IR ~/.config/systemd/user/ .
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on error (for example the mouse is missing, a value is out of
range or the profile is invalid), 2 on invalid command line usage.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
img339ctl dpi 1 1600            # first level: 1600 DPI
img339ctl dpi 6 \-\-off           # skip level 6
img339ctl color 2 ff8000        # level 2 orange
img339ctl led dpi               # show the current level's colour
img339ctl led off               # LEDs off
img339ctl button 4 dpi\-         # button 4 lowers the DPI
img339ctl macro hello text:hello enter
img339ctl button 5 macro:hello  # needs the daemon
img339ctl \-n reset              # show the factory settings as reports
.fi
.SH NOTES
Without the udev rule only root can open the mouse.
Install it with
.PP
.nf
sudo install \-m 644 contrib/70\-img339\-mouse.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/
sudo udevadm control \-\-reload\-rules
.fi
.PP
and plug the mouse in again.
.SH BUGS
Settings cannot be read from the mouse.
If they are changed by other software,
.B img339ctl apply
restores the profile.
.PP
The effect of the
.B fire
function has not been verified on hardware.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR img339ctl\-gui ,
the graphical version, which edits the same profile;
.BR hidraw (4),
.BR udev (7),
.BR systemd.unit (5)
